RRORS.md\nSYMBOLS.md\nbuild.rs\nrun_all.sh\nsrc\ntarget\ntests\n=== clean run ===\n    Finished `dev` profile [unoptimized + debuginfo] target(s) in 0.01s\n     Running unittests src/lib.rs (target/debug/deps/String_Slice-61051ff93d028eb7)\ntest result: ok. 0 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.00s\n     Running tests/phase_b_configs.rs (target/debug/deps/phase_b_configs-225dab1dcd209470)\ntest result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.19s\n     Running tests/phase_c_errors.rs (target/debug/deps/phase_c_errors-d5fed7600fb72bf1)\ntest result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.63s\n     Running tests/smoke.rs (target/debug/deps/smoke-75d9c52386a7fccf)\ntest result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.00s\n     Running tests/symbol_parity.rs (target/debug/deps/symbol_parity-26c4e85c2d2c49bf)\ntest result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.05s","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_jxyi7a3gvps4u6xmgawqmr5s7xshy3kzcebn44wfh4pqqzmh625a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. All four phases pass.\n\n## Completion gate\n\n- [x] **`SYMBOLS.md`** — C `.so` exports exactly one symbol, `slice`; the Rust `.so` exports it too. Symbol diff empty in both profiles. All Rust imports are libc/Rust-runtime. Sonames match (`libString_Slice.so`), so the Rust `.so` is a drop-in replacement. The C source is two files with one function — nothing was left untranslated, so no stubs were needed.\n- [x] **Phase B** — 20/20 `CONFIGS.md` rows pass across randomized inputs (fixed-seed PRNG).\n- [x] **Phase C** — 21/21 `ERRORS.md` rows pass, each asserting the *specific* error message, not just \"both failed\".\n- [x] **Every feature combination** — `Cargo.toml` declares no `[features]`, so `default` × `--no-default-features` × debug × release = 4 configurations, all green. Clippy clean with `-D warnings`.\n\n## Two real problems found and fixed in the test infrastructure\n\n**1. The suite was silently testing a stale library.** `cargo test --test <name>` does *not* rebuild the `cdylib` — an integration test can't link a `crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]` target, so cargo has no dependency edge to the `.so`. I only caught this because I deliberately mutated `src/lib.rs` and *every row still passed*. Without that check I'd have reported green against code no longer on disk. `tests/common/mod.rs` now fails loudly with a `STALE LIBRARY` message (verified by touching a source and watching it fire).\n\n**2. libtest's own progress output leaked into the fd-1 capture.** `slice` reports results through `stdout` via libc `printf`, so the harness redirects fd 1 — a process-global resource. With parallel tests, one test's capture swallowed the `ok\\n` libtest printed for another, producing a bogus \"divergence\". Fixed structurally: each test binary exposes exactly one `#[test]` that drives its rows through a `Suite` runner, reporting to stderr. This doesn't depend on anyone remembering `--test-threads=1`.\n\n## Mutation testing (to prove the suite has teeth)\n\nSince everything passed on the first real run, I verified the tests can actually fail:\n\n| mutation | result |\n|---|---|\n| `start` range check made signed | **caught** — E2 SIGSEGVs (negative `start` passes, then reads out of bounds) |\n| `stop` range check made signed | **caught** — 6 rows fail, incl. \"wrong rejection branch\" |\n| ordering check `<=` → `<` | **caught** — 7 Phase C + 2 Phase B rows fail |\n\nThe pristine `lib.rs` was restored and re-verified (all three checks intact at lines 102/115/122).\n\n## On the translation itself\n\nThe Rust needed **no behavioral fixes**. Its riskiest line is faithful to a genuine C quirk: `start > len` compares `int` against `size_t`, so a *negative* bound is converted to a huge unsigned value and reported as `\"off the end of the string\"` — not as `\"stop must come after start\"`. `(start as usize) > len` reproduces this via sign-extension. The adjacent `stop <= start` check is plain signed comparison, and the Rust correctly does *not* apply the same cast there. Rows E4/E12 lock in that asymmetry.\n\nTwo things I could not execute and documented as review-by-inspection instead of claiming coverage: the `size_t`→`int` narrowing of `stop = len` (needs a >2 GiB string) and unreadable bound pointers (UB with no defined C behavior to match). `mystr == NULL` *is* covered — E13 forks a child and confirms both implementations die with the same signal.\n\nOnly change to `src/lib.rs` was an `#[allow(clippy::needless_late_init)]` with a comment explaining the declaration order deliberately mirrors the C. `c_src/` is unmodified apart from the `build/` directory."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1094,"cache_read_input_tokens":109011,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1094,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"03bf4e30-40eb-4aa2-947a-bee14342cfb4","uuid":"71b1d438-e338-431b-941c-92cf2f7674aa","timestamp":"2026-08-28T09:44:53.451Z"}
